    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaungpaya.jpg" alt="Alaungpaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaungpaya.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phyo WP, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Konbaung dynasty arose in 1752 from the resistance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> to the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom, whose forces had just captured Ava and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Aung Zeya</a>, the headman of the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Moksobo</a> north of Ava, refused to swear allegiance to the southern conquerors, rallied the surrounding villages and proclaimed himself king under the name Alaungpaya, ‘future Buddha’. He renamed Moksobo Shwebo and made it his capital. In a series of rapid campaigns he drove the Hanthawaddy garrisons from Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, retaking Ava at the turn of 1753–1754, and then advanced into the delta. In 1755 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>, renaming it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> (Rangoon), ‘end of strife’; in 1756 he stormed Syriam, where French traders had supported the Mon; and in May 1757 he captured and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, ending the Hanthawaddy kingdom. Alaungpaya then subdued Manipur in 1758 and invaded Siam, but in 1760 he fell ill during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and died on the march home. Within eight years a village headman had reunited the whole Irrawaddy basin. His sons Naungdawgyi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyushin">Hsinbyushin</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> extended the realm into the most powerful state in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> of its time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mogok"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mogok.jpg" alt="Mogok" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mogok">Mogok</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mogok_City.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Channyeinhan-Mandalay, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Konbaung society was organised around the distinction between crown servicemen (<em>ahmudan</em>), who held land in return for military and labour service, and taxpaying commoners (<em>athi</em>), alongside debt-bondsmen and pagoda servants attached to religious foundations. Most people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers; the irrigated lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minbu">Minbu</a> fed the court, while the delta, depopulated by the wars of the 1750s, was gradually resettled. The kingdom exported teak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, rubies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogok">Mogok</a> and oil from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenangyaung">Yenangyaung</a>, and imported Indian textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and firearms. Overland trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in cotton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> revived after the war with the Qing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> grew as the main seaport until its loss to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1852. After that the kingdom was landlocked and dependent on river trade through British-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon</a> sought new revenue through royal monopolies and the <em>thathameda</em>, a household tax. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> was central to social and political life. Monasteries provided schooling to most boys, and the court sponsored reforms of the monastic order and great building projects, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a>’s gigantic unfinished pagoda at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingun-pahtodawgyi">Mingun</a> and Mindon’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuthodaw-pagoda">Kuthodaw Pagoda</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, where the Pali canon was inscribed on 729 marble slabs. Royal patronage also supported a rich court culture of drama, music and poetry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danubyu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-danubyu.jpg" alt="Battle of Danubyu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danubyu">Battle of Danubyu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Combined_Forces_under_Brig._Cotton,_C.B._and_Captains_Alexander,_C.B._%26_Chads,_R.N._passing_the_Fortress_of_Donabue_to_effect_a_junction_with_Sir_Archibald_Campbell,_on_the_27th_March_1825_1241485001_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Print made by: Henry Pyall After: Thomas Stothard After: Capt Thornton Published by: Thomas Clay, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Konbaung armies combined ahmudan regiments, provincial levies and Shan contingents with foreign specialists, including French gunners captured at Syriam in 1756, and they were capable of long campaigns far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>’s borders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyushin">Hsinbyushin</a> (r. 1763–1776) launched a two-pronged invasion of Siam that destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in April 1767, ending a kingdom that had lasted four centuries. At the same time Burma faced four invasions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> between 1765 and 1769; the Burmese defeated them all, destroying the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-rui">Ming Rui</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maymyo">Maymyo</a> in 1768 and surrounding the last Qing army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kaungton">Kaungton</a>, which led to a negotiated peace in 1769. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> (r. 1782–1819) conquered Arakan in 1784–1785 but lost a great invasion of Siam in 1785–1786. Burmese generals occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Manipur between 1817 and 1822, bringing Burma to the frontier of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> (1824–1826), in which the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-bandula">Maha Bandula</a> was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danubyu">Danubyu</a> in 1825, showed that Burmese armies could not match British artillery and steamships, and the wars of 1852 and 1885 were decided quickly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s favour. The army never fully modernised, despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon</a>’s efforts to acquire modern weapons and to train officers along European lines.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mindon-min.jpg" alt="Mindon Min" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon Min</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-Mindon_Min-2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ekyaw, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Konbaung kings ruled as Buddhist monarchs from palace cities laid out as cosmic centres, moving the capital several times, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Shwebo</a> to Sagaing, Ava and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>, and finally to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon</a> in 1857. Royal authority was exercised through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hluttaw">Hluttaw</a></em>, the council of ministers (<em>wungyi</em>), and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byedaik">Byedaik</a></em>, the inner privy council, with appointed governors (<em>myowun</em>) in the provinces and hereditary headmen (<em>myothugyi</em>) in the localities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> ordered revenue inquests (<em>sittan</em>) in the 1780s and in 1802 to record population, land and obligations, continuing a Toungoo practice. The Shan states, Manipur and other borderlands were ruled indirectly as tributaries. Succession was a recurring weakness: there was no fixed rule, and princes often fought or were executed when a king died. Mindon (r. 1853–1878), who came to power by deposing his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagan-min">Pagan Min</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a>, attempted a programme of modernisation. He introduced salaries for officials, a standard coinage, the thathameda tax, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> network and small factories, and sent missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His reforms faltered after the rebellion of 1866, in which his brother and heir, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanaung-mintha">Kanaung Prince</a>, a leading reformer, was assassinated by two of Mindon’s sons.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin.jpg" alt="Hmannan Yazawin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glass_Palace_Chronicle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hybernator, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Konbaung dynasty created the territorial core of modern Myanmar and much of its national memory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a>’s reunification ended Mon independence for good and confirmed Burman dominance of the Irrawaddy delta, while the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> created the city that became the colonial and later national capital. Konbaung wars reshaped mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>: the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767 cleared the way for the rise of Thonburi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> and the Chakri dynasty, and the Burmese victories over the Qing are remembered as one of the most successful defences against Chinese invasion. Burmese expansion into Arakan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Manipur brought the kingdom into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and the resulting Anglo-Burmese wars led to the incorporation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, with consequences that shaped the borders and ethnic politics of the region. Culturally, the period produced the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a></em> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gla">Glass</a> Palace Chronicle, 1832), legal compilations, court drama and poetry, and important Buddhist scholarship, including the Fifth Buddhist Council convened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon</a> in 1871. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahamuni-buddha">Mahamuni Buddha</a> image, brought from Arakan in 1785, remains one of the most venerated in the country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> remains the cultural capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Burmese War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_rangoon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J. Moore, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s decline was driven above all by conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Burmese expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Manipur, and border disputes over Arakan, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> of 1824–1826. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a> of February 1826 forced King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a> to cede Arakan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a>, renounce claims to Assam and Manipur and pay an indemnity of one million pounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a>. In 1852 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a>, provoked by disputes over British merchants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>, ended with the annexation of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, cutting the kingdom off from the sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon</a> accepted the loss without signing a treaty and sought to strengthen what remained, but the rump kingdom depended on the river trade with British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> and on teak, oil and ruby revenues increasingly exploited by foreign firms. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min">Thibaw</a> (r. 1878–1885) was placed on the throne by a palace faction led by the queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyumashin">Hsinbyumashin</a> and her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supayalat">Supayalat</a>, and the execution of dozens of royal relatives in 1879 damaged his reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In 1885 the court imposed a heavy fine on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-burmah-trading-corporation">Bombay Burmah Trading Corporation</a> over teak extraction, while British officials feared growing French influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. A British force advanced up the Irrawaddy in November 1885 and took Mandalay within a fortnight. Thibaw was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri-odisha">Ratnagiri</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> was annexed on 1 January 1886.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Konbaung dynasty reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in the 1750s, destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, defeated four Qing invasions and expanded into Arakan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Manipur, creating the core of modern Myanmar, but three wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> between 1824 and 1885 dismantled it and made Burma a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</em></p>

